<chapter xml:id="chp-accessibility-faq">
  <title>Accessibility: Frequently Asked Questions</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Accessibility is one of the main reasons instructors adopt <pretext />. This FAQ
      collects the questions that come up most often during the workshop, with short
      answers you can expand in conversation.
    </p>

    <p>
      <em>A one-sentence summary:</em> <pretext /> handles the infrastructure of
      accessibility — semantic structure, screen-reader-ready math, multiple output
      formats — so that the author's remaining work is focused, principally writing
      good alt text for images.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- =============================================================== -->
  <!-- What PreTeXt gives you automatically                            -->
  <!-- =============================================================== -->
  <section xml:id="faq-automatic">
    <title>What <pretext /> Does Automatically</title>

    <p>
      <dl>
        <li>
          <title>What accessibility features come for free?</title>
          <p>
            Because you mark content by <em>what it is</em> (definition, theorem, example)
            rather than how it looks, the HTML output has proper headings, landmarks, and
            ARIA roles built in. Screen-reader users can navigate by structure — jumping
            between sections, theorems, or examples — the same way a sighted reader skims.
            Keyboard navigation and color-independent styling are also included by default.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>How is mathematics made accessible?</title>
          <p>
            <pretext /> relies on MathJax, which renders math in a form that screen
            readers can read intelligently. Right-clicking any equation brings up an
            accessibility menu where readers can turn on the Explorer to navigate
            expressions piece by piece — numerator, exponent, summand — rather than
            hearing a flat string of symbols.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>What output formats does <pretext /> produce?</title>
          <p>
            From one source: HTML (the most accessible format), PDF, EPUB, and braille.
            A student who needs a specific format is not getting a second-class version
            of the text — every format is generated from the same semantic source.
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </p>

    <assemblage xml:id="faq-demo-tip">
      <title>Live demo move</title>
      <p>
        If accessibility comes up during a demo, right-click an equation in the HTML
        output, open the MathJax context menu, and turn on
        <term>Accessibility → Explorer → Activate</term>. The page reloads and the
        reader can now navigate the equation's structure with arrow keys. Fifteen
        seconds, immediate payoff.
      </p>
    </assemblage>
  </section>

  <!-- =============================================================== -->
  <!-- What the author has to do                                       -->
  <!-- =============================================================== -->
  <section xml:id="faq-author-work">
    <title>What the Author Still Has to Do</title>

    <p>
      <dl>
        <li>
          <title>What's my actual responsibility as an author?</title>
          <p>
            Write alt text for images, and design images so they work without color.
            That is essentially the whole list. The structural markup you are learning
            in this workshop is already the accessibility practice — you do not need
            a separate accessibility pass at the end.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>How do I write alt text in <pretext />?</title>
          <p>
            Every non-decorative image needs a <c>&lt;shortdescription&gt;</c>: plain
            text, no markup, no quotation marks, under about 125 characters. Complex
            images can also include a <c>&lt;description&gt;</c> with paragraphs and
            even math, which screen readers read as a longer alternative.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>Why can't <pretext /> generate alt text automatically?</title>
          <p>
            Because image descriptions depend on author intent and context. The same
            graph might be described one way in a section about asymptotes and another
            way in a section about symmetry. No tool can reliably make that call.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>What about using color in figures?</title>
          <p>
            Don't rely on color alone. If you ask students to compare the red and green
            curves, add a second distinguishing feature — dashing, thickness, or labels —
            so the figure still makes sense in grayscale or to a colorblind reader.
            A quick test: view your image in black and white.
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- =============================================================== -->
  <!-- Braille                                                         -->
  <!-- =============================================================== -->
  <section xml:id="faq-braille">
    <title>Braille and Tactile Output</title>

    <p>
      <dl>
        <li>
          <title>Does <pretext /> really produce braille?</title>
          <p>
            Yes, from the same source file. Formulas are rendered in Nemeth braille,
            and tactile diagrams can be generated automatically from structured
            figures. The approach has been validated on full-length textbooks in
            abstract algebra and calculus, with quality checked by a certified
            transcriber and readability confirmed by a blind mathematician.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>If screen readers can read math aloud, why does braille still matter?</title>
          <p>
            Because mathematicians read math by looking back and forth — comparing
            pieces, holding sub-expressions in view. Braille gives a blind reader the
            same tactile access: scan, return, compare. Audio narration of a formula
            is no more sufficient for a blind reader than it would be for a sighted
            one.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>Is the braille pipeline part of today's workshop?</title>
          <p>
            No. Producing braille involves additional tools beyond the Codespaces
            setup we're using. The honest framing is that the capability exists and
            is mature, and the HTML you build today is already screen-reader
            accessible. Braille is a next step for instructors who need it.
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- =============================================================== -->
  <!-- Comparison to LaTeX / PDF                                       -->
  <!-- =============================================================== -->
  <section xml:id="faq-comparison">
    <title>Comparison with <latex /> and PDF</title>

    <p>
      <dl>
        <li>
          <title>Isn't PDF from <latex /> accessible enough?</title>
          <p>
            Accessible PDFs are possible but genuinely difficult to produce well from
            <latex /> — it takes extra tooling and careful tagging, and the result is
            often imperfect. HTML is fundamentally a more accessible format: it
            reflows, works natively with screen readers, and adapts to user
            preferences for font size and contrast.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>What makes <pretext /> structurally different?</title>
          <p>
            When you write <c>\textbf{Definition}</c> in <latex />, the document
            knows only that the text should be bold. When you write
            <c>&lt;definition&gt;</c> in <pretext />, every output format knows what
            the element <em>is</em> and can render it appropriately — including
            announcing it correctly to a screen reader.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>Can I convert my existing <latex /> course to <pretext />?</title>
          <p>
            Partially. <pretext />.Plus includes a <latex />-to-<pretext /> converter,
            but treat its output as a starting point rather than a finished
            conversion. Image descriptions in particular need to be added by hand.
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- =============================================================== -->
  <!-- Compliance and institutional concerns                           -->
  <!-- =============================================================== -->
  <section xml:id="faq-compliance">
    <title>Compliance and Institutional Questions</title>

    <p>
      <dl>
        <li>
          <title>Is <pretext /> WCAG-compliant?</title>
          <p>
            <pretext />'s HTML output is designed to meet WCAG standards, and the
            community takes this seriously. But compliance is a property of a specific
            document, not a tool — if an author skips alt text or writes bad
            descriptions, no tool can compensate. <pretext /> plus good authoring
            habits gets you there.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>Does this help with recent accessibility requirements?</title>
          <p>
            Yes. Many US institutions are now required to ensure accessibility of all
            online materials, including content posted in LMSs like Canvas or
            Blackboard. <pretext /> is well-positioned for this: you can deploy the
            HTML publicly, or export a single portable HTML file that uploads
            directly to an LMS.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>What about EPUB for students who want to read on a tablet or e-reader?</title>
          <p>
            Produced from the same source. EPUB reflows, supports screen readers,
            and works across the major e-reader apps.
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- =============================================================== -->
  <!-- Harder questions worth pre-thinking                             -->
  <!-- =============================================================== -->
  <section xml:id="faq-harder">
    <title>Harder Questions</title>

    <p>
      <dl>
        <li>
          <title>Are interactive elements accessible?</title>
          <p>
            Native <pretext /> exercise types — multiple choice, true/false, fill-in —
            are generally accessible. Embedded third-party interactives (GeoGebra,
            Desmos, DoenetML) inherit the accessibility of those platforms, which
            varies. For critical content, consider including an image or text
            description as a fallback.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>Do I need to learn accessibility standards to use this well?</title>
          <p>
            No. The workshop's authoring practices — semantic structure, short
            descriptions for images, color-independent figures — <em>are</em> the
            accessibility practice. You don't need to study WCAG to produce
            accessible output with <pretext />.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>Can I see a real accessible <pretext /> textbook?</title>
          <p>
            Tom Judson's <em>Abstract Algebra: Theory and Applications</em> is a
            well-known example and works well as a live demo. The American Institute
            of Mathematics maintains a list of open textbooks at
            <url href="https://aimath.org/textbooks/">aimath.org/textbooks</url>,
            many authored in <pretext />.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>Who decides what "accessible enough" means for my course?</title>
          <p>
            Ultimately your institution and your students. <pretext /> gives you a
            document that is accessible by default and easy to improve where it
            matters most. If you have a specific student with a specific
            accommodation, work with your campus disability services — the HTML
            and EPUB outputs will cover the vast majority of common needs out of the
            box.
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </p>
  </section>

</chapter>
